In this lesson, we'll look at solving polynomial equations the clean, reliable way, and the trick is almost always the same, factorise and use the null factor law. First, you rearrange everything so that one side equals zero. Then you factorise the polynomial as fully as you can. Pull out any common factor first, then for cubics you use the factor theorem to spot a linear factor. Once it's fully factorised, the null factor law says that if a product of things equals zero, then at least one of those things must be zero, so you set each factor equal to zero and solve each little equation on its own. For quadratics, factorise if you can, and if you can't, reach for the quadratic formula. For cubics, you test the factors of the constant term to find one root, divide it out to leave a quadratic, and then solve that. The order matters, zero first, factorise second, and solve last. Now let's see it in action.